1
00:00:03,092 --> 00:00:06,555
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is the Middle Length Discourse, number 40.

2
00:00:07,917 --> 00:00:10,059
[SPEAKER_00]: The Shorter Discourse at Asapuddha.

3
00:00:13,882 --> 00:00:21,189
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha goes on saying, recluses, recluses, because that is how people perceive you.

4
00:00:21,249 --> 00:00:22,871
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you are asked, what are you?

5
00:00:23,331 --> 00:00:24,793
[SPEAKER_00]: You claim that you are recluses.

6
00:00:26,873 --> 00:00:30,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Since that is what you are designated and what you claim to be, you should train thus.

7
00:00:31,834 --> 00:00:52,579
[SPEAKER_00]: We will practice the way proper to the recluse, so that our designations may be true and our claims genuine, and so that the services of those whose robes, alms food, resting place and medicines we use, shall bring them great fruit and benefit, and so that our going forth shall not be vain, but fruitful and fertile.

8
00:00:56,291 --> 00:01:01,297
[SPEAKER_00]: And how does a bhikkhu not practice the way proper to the rec... to the recluse?

9
00:01:02,698 --> 00:01:17,614
[SPEAKER_00]: For so long as a bhikkhu who is covetous, has not abandoned covetousness, who has a mind of ill will... ill will, and has not abandoned ill will, who has a mind of full with defilements, and has not abandoned defilements,

10
00:01:27,936 --> 00:01:42,700
[SPEAKER_00]: I say because of his failure to abandon these stains for the recluse, these faults for the recluse, which are grounds for rebirth in a state of deprivation and whose results are to be experienced in an unhappy destination.

11
00:01:50,448 --> 00:02:02,799
[SPEAKER_00]: I do not say that the recluse's status comes about in a rag-robe wearer, through the mere wearing of rag-robes, nor in, well, basically any ascetic practice.

12
00:02:03,620 --> 00:02:09,766
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't become an ascetic through... You don't become a recluse through asceticism.

13
00:02:09,926 --> 00:02:10,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Through asceticism, yeah.

14
00:02:11,387 --> 00:02:12,508
[SPEAKER_00]: By standing on one leg.

15
00:02:12,808 --> 00:02:12,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

16
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:40,769
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it is, if through the mere wearing of rag robes, one who is a rag robe wearer, who was covetousness, covetous, abandoned covetousness, then his friends and companions and kinsmen and relatives would make him a rag robe as soon as he was born and have him undertake the rag robe wearing.

17
00:02:40,789 --> 00:02:41,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus,

18
00:02:42,878 --> 00:02:50,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Come, my dear, be a rag-robe wearer, so that by mere wearing of a rag-robe, when you are defiled, you will abandon defilement.

19
00:02:50,683 --> 00:02:52,304
[SPEAKER_00]: When you have wrong view, you abandon wrong view.

20
00:02:55,667 --> 00:02:57,668
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be that easy.

21
00:02:57,688 --> 00:02:58,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

22
00:02:58,408 --> 00:03:03,412
[SPEAKER_01]: The work would be done for you, through wearing the... just wearing the rag-robes.

23
00:03:04,192 --> 00:03:05,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so going...

24
00:03:11,350 --> 00:03:14,193
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and how does a bhikkhu practice the way proper to a recluse?

25
00:03:14,494 --> 00:03:25,187
[SPEAKER_00]: When a bhikkhu who has a defilement, abandons the defilement, he has wrong view and he abandons wrong view, then he practices the way proper to the recluse.

26
00:03:26,334 --> 00:03:29,977
[SPEAKER_00]: He sees himself purified of all these evil, unwholesome states.

27
00:03:30,137 --> 00:03:31,718
[SPEAKER_00]: He sees himself liberated from them.

28
00:03:32,399 --> 00:03:34,480
[SPEAKER_00]: When he sees this, gladness is born in him.

29
00:03:35,241 --> 00:03:36,942
[SPEAKER_00]: When he's glad, rapture is born in him.

30
00:03:37,683 --> 00:03:41,866
[SPEAKER_00]: In one who is rapturous, enjoys, the body becomes tranquil.

31
00:03:41,886 --> 00:03:53,975
[SPEAKER_01]: See, that very thing you just read, kind of, on one hand, it sounds obvious, self-explanatory,

32
00:03:55,707 --> 00:03:58,849
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, on the other hand, nobody seems to do it.

33
00:04:00,130 --> 00:04:10,217
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, like, even people who are committed to the practice, they don't see, directly, that these states are abandoned, and then their gladness results.

34
00:04:10,277 --> 00:04:15,661
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all based on some sort of faith, approximation of, I'll do this, then this will happen.

35
00:04:16,441 --> 00:04:18,963
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll practice this, then I might have experience of that.

36
00:04:19,814 --> 00:04:24,497
[SPEAKER_01]: But a direct knowledge, recognition of this is wholesome, this is unwholesome.

37
00:04:25,017 --> 00:04:29,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Unwholesome is abandoned, because of that I'm glad, and raptured, and so on.

38
00:04:30,380 --> 00:04:31,281
[SPEAKER_01]: That's completely absent.

39
00:04:32,582 --> 00:04:37,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Although, it's an obvious passage, like you can read it, as you just did, and okay, yeah, of course.

40
00:04:38,125 --> 00:04:42,507
[SPEAKER_01]: But not many people would recognize that's all to it, in a way.

41
00:04:43,028 --> 00:04:45,149
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all the work that needs to be done.

42
00:04:45,269 --> 00:04:46,870
[SPEAKER_01]: It's within that line you just read.

43
00:04:48,330 --> 00:04:51,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Not elsewhere, not before or after, or in some different moods.

44
00:04:51,994 --> 00:04:54,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding unwholesome, and then abandoning it.

45
00:04:55,577 --> 00:05:00,942
[SPEAKER_01]: And then knowing there are no unwholesome states present, and that gives rise automatically to gladness.

46
00:05:01,503 --> 00:05:03,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you feel like you've done the work, the right work.

47
00:05:06,223 --> 00:05:20,074
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the only reason, natural conclusion is, the only reason why people wouldn't be seeing that and would be doing all sorts of other practices and methods and techniques and faiths and traditions and whatnot, is because they don't understand what wholesome is, nor unwholesome.

48
00:05:20,734 --> 00:05:21,755
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no understanding of it.

49
00:05:22,716 --> 00:05:25,138
[SPEAKER_00]: First thing, if you ask someone, do you know what ill-will is?

50
00:05:25,678 --> 00:05:25,858
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

51
00:05:25,878 --> 00:05:26,759
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes or no?

52
00:05:26,779 --> 00:05:28,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what I mean.

53
00:05:28,340 --> 00:05:29,881
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the whole point, is...

54
00:05:31,763 --> 00:05:35,706
[SPEAKER_01]: But putujana needs to recognize putujana, which means his

55
00:05:36,345 --> 00:05:38,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowledge of ill-will is not the right knowledge.

56
00:05:39,346 --> 00:05:41,966
[SPEAKER_01]: His knowledge of non-ill-will is not the right knowledge.

57
00:05:42,126 --> 00:05:47,707
[SPEAKER_01]: His knowledge of sensuality, of non-sensuality, his knowledge of right view and wrong view, are not the right knowledges.

58
00:05:49,248 --> 00:05:56,009
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, nobody denies that somebody who started a practice, or been practicing for a long time, but still a bhutujjana, doesn't know anything.

59
00:05:58,070 --> 00:05:59,870
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if they're still not sotāpanna,

60
00:06:00,484 --> 00:06:04,907
[SPEAKER_01]: They themselves should not regard their knowledge as valid.

61
00:06:05,808 --> 00:06:15,976
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that, they will keep making effort further, until they verify it with their own, like, basic understanding of freedom from suffering.

62
00:06:16,016 --> 00:06:16,756
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, exactly.

63
00:06:16,896 --> 00:06:19,178
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what every person should be questioning themselves.

64
00:06:20,239 --> 00:06:20,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, wait a minute.

65
00:06:20,940 --> 00:06:29,286
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm reading all these suttas about ill-will, and here I am talking, like, I should do this, I should do that, but do we even know what a phenomenon of ill-will is?

66
00:06:30,204 --> 00:06:34,809
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I designating when I say ill will?

67
00:06:34,849 --> 00:06:35,750
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I referring to?

68
00:06:35,890 --> 00:06:36,270
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it?

69
00:06:36,871 --> 00:06:37,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it a state of mind?

70
00:06:37,792 --> 00:06:38,172
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it?

71
00:06:39,073 --> 00:06:41,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Just don't look for information to stand for the answer.

72
00:06:42,296 --> 00:06:50,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Just ask yourself, what is it in your very experience at the same time that you are pointing at when you say this ill will?

73
00:06:51,926 --> 00:06:52,367
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it?

74
00:06:53,191 --> 00:06:54,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it a pressure in your chest?

75
00:06:54,692 --> 00:06:55,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it a redness in your face?

76
00:06:55,993 --> 00:07:00,216
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, if you look closely, you realize, well, it can't be that, because those are the results of the ill-will.

77
00:07:00,777 --> 00:07:02,178
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the ill-will?

78
00:07:03,579 --> 00:07:21,773
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's... like... people think that putujana can become sotāpanna through, kind of, upgrading their understanding, you know, getting the... the more accurate truth based on what you... what you, kind of, know about the life, getting... getting greater insights into higher...

79
00:07:22,880 --> 00:07:24,201
[SPEAKER_00]: Universal nature of things.

80
00:07:24,581 --> 00:07:26,122
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's completely the wrong direction.

81
00:07:26,903 --> 00:07:32,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Pūtujana can become Sotāpanna when he completely undoes everything that makes him Pūtujana.

82
00:07:32,727 --> 00:07:42,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Not when he builds on top of it the new ideas and new views and new experiences, but when he really undoes all of it that he is.

83
00:07:42,574 --> 00:07:48,758
[SPEAKER_01]: With instruction from the suttas, that state, then, is where the right view can take place.

84
00:07:51,059 --> 00:08:01,024
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for example, if someone says, is my mind filled with ill-will?

85
00:08:02,725 --> 00:08:03,485
[SPEAKER_00]: What is ill-will?

86
00:08:04,446 --> 00:08:04,566
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

87
00:08:04,946 --> 00:08:06,047
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in order... exactly.

88
00:08:06,067 --> 00:08:09,688
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you cannot... am I... am I... am I engaging in ill-will now?

89
00:08:09,728 --> 00:08:11,729
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say yes or no, but then you go further.

90
00:08:11,769 --> 00:08:15,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, but... you'll understand what ill-will is.

91
00:08:15,431 --> 00:08:16,732
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I asking myself here?

92
00:08:20,482 --> 00:08:29,187
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a lack of that kind of right reflection on the basic nature of designation and terms that you already use, that a bhaktuṣa already uses.

93
00:08:29,588 --> 00:08:31,689
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he keeps maintaining his wrong views.

94
00:08:32,329 --> 00:08:38,813
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because he hasn't make... made enough effort invested in some technique or... or practice of restraint.

95
00:08:39,474 --> 00:08:40,454
[SPEAKER_01]: He probably made too much.

96
00:08:42,215 --> 00:08:47,819
[SPEAKER_01]: But none of that can make any fundamental difference, because he hasn't uprooted.

97
00:08:52,086 --> 00:08:58,668
[SPEAKER_01]: He just took aspects of those views for granted and then started his practice as building further on top of it.

98
00:08:59,228 --> 00:09:04,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, no matter how many stories you built, you built it on completely improper foundations.

99
00:09:04,330 --> 00:09:10,952
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't go as sufficiently high, because foundations can't support it.

100
00:09:12,741 --> 00:09:19,204
[SPEAKER_01]: So, instead of trying to find out the answers, you really need to understand what do you... what are you even asking?

101
00:09:20,905 --> 00:09:22,905
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you referring to when you ask these questions?

102
00:09:23,346 --> 00:09:24,766
[SPEAKER_01]: And ill-will was just one example.

103
00:09:24,866 --> 00:09:26,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Just take any of the aggregates.

104
00:09:26,287 --> 00:09:27,747
[SPEAKER_01]: Ask yourself about your own feelings.

105
00:09:29,668 --> 00:09:32,509
[SPEAKER_01]: When you say, oh, I'm feeling like this today.

106
00:09:33,730 --> 00:09:35,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, don't worry about the content of it.

107
00:09:35,711 --> 00:09:40,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Just think about the designation of word, feeling, that you used.

108
00:09:41,482 --> 00:09:47,186
[SPEAKER_01]: In whatever language you would have used it, and ask yourself, okay, but what am I... what am I referring to?

109
00:09:47,906 --> 00:09:48,887
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I pointing at?

110
00:09:49,007 --> 00:09:54,151
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I designating with this designation of my language in this experience?

111
00:09:54,871 --> 00:09:58,393
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is that feeling that I just said, it's such and such?

112
00:09:59,834 --> 00:10:00,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I describe it?

113
00:10:00,755 --> 00:10:01,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I understand it?

114
00:10:01,596 --> 00:10:02,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I see its nature?

115
00:10:04,057 --> 00:10:05,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, no, the feeling is impermanent.

116
00:10:05,358 --> 00:10:06,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's all great.

117
00:10:06,519 --> 00:10:07,500
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all what you heard.

118
00:10:08,100 --> 00:10:11,042
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all your information, your third-person view, your theory.

119
00:10:12,006 --> 00:10:18,069
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is necessary to have, but if you stay on the level of it, it can never really result in an insight that matters.

120
00:10:20,310 --> 00:10:27,834
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to see, for you, at the time of saying, my feeling is such and such, what do you refer to when you say that feeling?

121
00:10:29,735 --> 00:10:30,096
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it?

122
00:10:30,816 --> 00:10:37,840
[SPEAKER_01]: How far you can designate it, how far you can discern it, without... then everything else you think about a feeling is put it aside,

123
00:10:38,641 --> 00:10:42,704
[SPEAKER_01]: First, start seeing what is being experienced as a feeling, when you say feeling.

124
00:10:44,686 --> 00:10:51,050
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when the suttas, when sometimes, when the monks would try to understand something, they would say it out loud, like, like, oh, feeling, feeling, what is feeling?

125
00:10:51,110 --> 00:10:53,212
[SPEAKER_01]: Or first jhāna, first jhāna, what is first jhāna?

126
00:10:53,572 --> 00:10:54,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Say it out loud.

127
00:10:54,533 --> 00:10:55,734
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you bring it to your mind.

128
00:10:56,774 --> 00:10:58,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Once, twice, three times.

129
00:10:58,616 --> 00:11:06,402
[SPEAKER_01]: And, okay, so now, this enduring experience of having just said this out loud, within this enduring experience, I can

130
00:11:07,365 --> 00:11:10,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Hopefully, start discerning the direction where that feeling actually is.

131
00:11:11,007 --> 00:11:11,868
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I referring to?

132
00:11:12,869 --> 00:11:20,474
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize that any of the five aggregates, or all of the five aggregates, are muddled in your own understanding, in regard to your own five aggregates.

133
00:11:21,314 --> 00:11:29,520
[SPEAKER_01]: So, people go through their life using words like matter, feeling, my perception, or my consciousness, my intentions, but the understanding of it, it's completely muddled.

134
00:11:30,275 --> 00:11:35,437
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a concoction of various concepts and ideas, never questioned, often self-contradictory.

135
00:11:35,957 --> 00:11:39,798
[SPEAKER_01]: And on top of it then, you try to practice the dhamma, as in keep adding more.

136
00:11:41,358 --> 00:11:47,160
[SPEAKER_01]: But the right practice of dhamma is undoing the ignorance, not adding the right knowledge on top of the ignorance.

137
00:11:48,221 --> 00:11:49,081
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not the knowledge.

138
00:11:50,340 --> 00:11:54,362
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you undo by reverting back to questioning the basic nature of it.

139
00:11:54,782 --> 00:11:56,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Not denying it, not affirming it.

140
00:11:56,563 --> 00:12:02,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Just putting it in question, so that you can see what is there, enduring, that you just refer to as feeling.

141
00:12:05,412 --> 00:12:05,952
[SPEAKER_01]: What is there?

142
00:12:05,972 --> 00:12:08,293
[SPEAKER_01]: No, there's no feeling, there's nothing, there's nothing, nothing's permanent.

143
00:12:08,313 --> 00:12:14,235
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another third-person view, assumption in regard to your own experience, that you just jump into out of your views and ideas.

144
00:12:14,955 --> 00:12:19,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Put all that aside, ask yourself, feeling, feeling, what is my feeling right here, right now?

145
00:12:19,737 --> 00:12:20,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'm feeling pleasant.

146
00:12:20,857 --> 00:12:21,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

147
00:12:21,478 --> 00:12:26,820
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that designation, feeling pleasant, what is the feeling aspect of that pleasant?

148
00:12:28,080 --> 00:12:29,060
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you refer to?

149
00:12:29,100 --> 00:12:33,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if there was nothing to refer to, you wouldn't use that word as a designation.

150
00:12:34,336 --> 00:12:39,220
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be just a hollow sound, just a pointless sound, and we don't use pointless sounds like that.

151
00:12:40,500 --> 00:12:51,849
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even when there's something not understood, in the case of Puthujjana, the nature is aggregate, it's completely muddled, eh... it still refers to something, and that's the starting point.

152
00:12:52,269 --> 00:12:58,013
[SPEAKER_01]: What is that something you are referring to, when you say, my perception?

153
00:12:58,553 --> 00:13:01,976
[SPEAKER_00]: If I just go back to that, your will, again, like if I say it,

154
00:13:02,922 --> 00:13:05,084
[SPEAKER_00]: If I question, is my mind defiled?

155
00:13:05,164 --> 00:13:06,405
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I mean by defilement?

156
00:13:06,425 --> 00:13:06,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

157
00:13:07,026 --> 00:13:17,596
[SPEAKER_01]: In order to question yourself rightly... See, the Buddha said that the monk will do that, which means he already has undone all these wrong views about what ill will is, what defilement is.

158
00:13:17,616 --> 00:13:22,141
[SPEAKER_01]: He already knows these things, so then that kind of questioning can be right and productive.

159
00:13:23,162 --> 00:13:24,643
[SPEAKER_01]: But yes, you can start.

160
00:13:24,743 --> 00:13:25,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Is my mind defiled?

161
00:13:25,824 --> 00:13:27,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so what is then defilement?

162
00:13:28,198 --> 00:13:30,319
[SPEAKER_01]: What do I mean by defilement?

163
00:13:30,640 --> 00:13:35,663
[SPEAKER_01]: I know what defilement means in theory, in the dictionary definition, in the Pali Text Society dictionary.

164
00:13:36,323 --> 00:13:37,384
[SPEAKER_01]: I know what defilement is.

165
00:13:37,904 --> 00:13:40,286
[SPEAKER_01]: But what is it for me when I say defilement?

166
00:13:40,386 --> 00:13:41,267
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I referring to?

167
00:13:41,807 --> 00:13:46,570
[SPEAKER_01]: And then look at what you're referring to and see if it matches what you think defilement is.

168
00:13:48,091 --> 00:13:56,176
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you start to undo the layers of this muddled thinking in regard to the basic phenomena of your experience, the nature of phenomena of your experience.

169
00:13:59,012 --> 00:13:59,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

170
00:13:59,552 --> 00:14:01,733
[SPEAKER_00]: In terms of, like, ill-will again, so I'll go back to...

171
00:14:03,254 --> 00:14:08,037
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like the resistance in regard to something, to a feeling.

172
00:14:08,637 --> 00:14:08,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

173
00:14:09,117 --> 00:14:10,558
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you need to know what feeling is.

174
00:14:10,778 --> 00:14:11,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

175
00:14:12,619 --> 00:14:16,381
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you look what ill-will is... Well, that's how you also know it.

176
00:14:16,401 --> 00:14:18,302
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't think, like, I need to know this before I can do this.

177
00:14:18,342 --> 00:14:18,442
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

178
00:14:18,462 --> 00:14:19,263
[SPEAKER_01]: You start doing it.

179
00:14:19,723 --> 00:14:21,304
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you realize how much you don't know.

180
00:14:21,964 --> 00:14:25,266
[SPEAKER_01]: By knowing how much you don't know, you know exactly how much you need to learn.

181
00:14:26,226 --> 00:14:27,607
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't start the questioning,

182
00:14:27,972 --> 00:14:29,533
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't even know how much you don't know.

183
00:14:30,474 --> 00:14:33,797
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the worst type of ignorance.

184
00:14:33,817 --> 00:14:34,318
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah.

185
00:14:35,239 --> 00:14:35,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Ill-will.

186
00:14:35,719 --> 00:14:36,900
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I referring to as ill-will?

187
00:14:36,960 --> 00:14:37,881
[SPEAKER_01]: How would I describe it?

188
00:14:39,783 --> 00:14:42,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, first and obvious, it's unpleasant.

189
00:14:43,366 --> 00:14:43,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

190
00:14:43,706 --> 00:14:44,907
[SPEAKER_01]: So, ill-will is unpleasant.

191
00:14:47,169 --> 00:14:49,011
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a resistance towards it.

192
00:14:49,111 --> 00:14:49,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

193
00:14:49,632 --> 00:14:50,352
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can take further.

194
00:14:50,613 --> 00:14:52,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you can go into, like, psychologizing mode.

195
00:14:53,889 --> 00:14:57,992
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, that's you assuming the third-person view in regard to your own experience of your world.

196
00:14:58,472 --> 00:14:59,413
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to do that.

197
00:15:00,193 --> 00:15:08,278
[SPEAKER_01]: What you want to do is repeating the question, if your mind won't stay on it, if it hasn't been learned, if it hasn't been taught how to think properly.

198
00:15:09,059 --> 00:15:18,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Keep repeating it and keep dwelling on the topic without giving in to self, kind of, in... fabricated third-person explanations based on the theory you read.

199
00:15:20,285 --> 00:15:21,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Nor do you mystify it.

200
00:15:21,445 --> 00:15:23,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, ill-will is inexplicable.

201
00:15:23,326 --> 00:15:25,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you just explain it by calling it inexplicable.

202
00:15:26,006 --> 00:15:27,266
[SPEAKER_01]: That is clearly its property, then.

203
00:15:27,886 --> 00:15:29,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means it's not truly inexplicable.

204
00:15:29,327 --> 00:15:31,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means your own designation contradicted itself.

205
00:15:32,567 --> 00:15:33,587
[SPEAKER_01]: So, those are the criteria.

206
00:15:33,627 --> 00:15:34,628
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't contradict yourself.

207
00:15:35,468 --> 00:15:36,848
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't assume the third-person view.

208
00:15:39,089 --> 00:15:40,869
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how else would you then describe the ill-will?

209
00:15:41,509 --> 00:15:44,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Just not wanting... Well, that's it.

210
00:15:44,450 --> 00:15:44,650
[SPEAKER_01]: See?

211
00:15:45,310 --> 00:15:45,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Not wanting.

212
00:15:46,590 --> 00:15:47,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Not wanting.

213
00:15:47,703 --> 00:15:49,845
[SPEAKER_01]: And we said, what's the basic characteristic of ill-will?

214
00:15:49,885 --> 00:15:50,525
[SPEAKER_01]: It's unpleasant.

215
00:15:51,006 --> 00:15:52,447
[SPEAKER_01]: So, which one is it that you don't want?

216
00:15:53,367 --> 00:15:55,569
[SPEAKER_01]: The unpleasantness of ill-will or the ill-will?

217
00:16:00,553 --> 00:16:01,293
[SPEAKER_00]: The unpleasantness.

218
00:16:01,513 --> 00:16:02,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

219
00:16:03,075 --> 00:16:06,237
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize if, say, theoretically, ill-will would not be unpleasant.

220
00:16:07,238 --> 00:16:09,319
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how when people say they have anger problem.

221
00:16:10,060 --> 00:16:15,224
[SPEAKER_01]: They have anger problem when they start basically, like, bitterly indulging.

222
00:16:17,283 --> 00:16:30,716
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in order for them to undo that ill-will, they first need to stop enjoying it and see it for what it is, which is unpleasant.

223
00:16:32,137 --> 00:16:38,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's because it's unpleasant that you... Well, you have to deal with it because it's unpleasant.

224
00:16:38,363 --> 00:16:39,244
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to get rid of it.

225
00:16:39,895 --> 00:16:42,736
[SPEAKER_01]: You already get rid of it, you're fine enjoying it, you get rid of it by dismissing it.

226
00:16:42,756 --> 00:16:48,117
[SPEAKER_00]: So, engaging in ill-will is a form of pleasure?

227
00:16:48,197 --> 00:16:49,878
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a form of trying to escape the pain.

228
00:16:50,018 --> 00:16:50,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

229
00:16:50,478 --> 00:16:50,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

230
00:16:54,099 --> 00:16:59,541
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first and foremost then, if you want to overcome ill-will, you have to stop acting habitually.

231
00:17:00,221 --> 00:17:06,002
[SPEAKER_01]: And the habit in this case, as we just clarified, is trying to avoid unpleasant feeling, displeasure.

232
00:17:07,083 --> 00:17:08,003
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's all it takes.

233
00:17:09,331 --> 00:17:14,854
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are able to hold the unpleasant feeling for as long as it endures, you won't give in to ill-will.

234
00:17:15,154 --> 00:17:16,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you have no anger problem.

235
00:17:18,455 --> 00:17:20,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger problem is when you can't restrain yourself.

236
00:17:22,777 --> 00:17:26,579
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, all of this now, that we just described, came from basic questioning.

237
00:17:26,799 --> 00:17:28,520
[SPEAKER_01]: What is ill-will, in my experience?

238
00:17:28,960 --> 00:17:32,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Not what is ill-will for me, as in it can mean something else for somebody else.

239
00:17:32,182 --> 00:17:32,342
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

240
00:17:32,762 --> 00:17:36,364
[SPEAKER_01]: What are the universal characteristics of the phenomenon of ill-will?

241
00:17:37,965 --> 00:17:38,665
[SPEAKER_01]: It's unpleasant.

242
00:17:39,355 --> 00:17:41,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, there is resistance in regard to that displeasure.

243
00:17:43,058 --> 00:17:46,721
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that, you then give in to the proposed actions of your real will.

244
00:17:47,941 --> 00:17:57,308
[SPEAKER_01]: They not just distract you from displeasure, but offer certain, like, amount of pleasurable just... justified pleasure through, like, oh, well, justice has been served, you know.

245
00:17:58,229 --> 00:17:59,730
[SPEAKER_01]: He done me... he done me harm.

246
00:17:59,810 --> 00:17:59,930
[SPEAKER_01]: I...

247
00:17:59,990 --> 00:18:00,691
[SPEAKER_01]: I retaliated.

248
00:18:00,711 --> 00:18:01,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I feel good.

249
00:18:01,451 --> 00:18:02,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Or things like that.

250
00:18:02,372 --> 00:18:06,115
[SPEAKER_01]: Or just... just generally letting it out there, affecting others.

251
00:18:06,777 --> 00:18:07,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Great.

252
00:18:08,078 --> 00:18:14,881
[SPEAKER_01]: So, abandon ill-will.

253
00:18:15,541 --> 00:18:16,701
[SPEAKER_00]: He abandons ill-will.

254
00:18:17,642 --> 00:18:21,323
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what motivates ill-will, then, is running away from your own displeasure.

255
00:18:21,944 --> 00:18:27,306
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to unmotivate the act of ill-will, stop running away from your own displeasure.

256
00:18:28,033 --> 00:18:29,353
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no outlet for your will, then.

257
00:18:29,614 --> 00:18:30,074
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely.

258
00:18:30,494 --> 00:18:31,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Cut at its root.

259
00:18:31,914 --> 00:18:33,235
[SPEAKER_00]: And for, like, envy.

260
00:18:33,295 --> 00:18:35,055
[SPEAKER_00]: It's mentioned here.

261
00:18:35,195 --> 00:18:35,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Envy.

262
00:18:35,796 --> 00:18:36,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, same.

263
00:18:36,836 --> 00:18:38,917
[SPEAKER_01]: What is envy in its fundamental characteristic?

264
00:18:38,977 --> 00:18:40,097
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it pleasant or unpleasant?

265
00:18:40,537 --> 00:18:41,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, it's unpleasant.

266
00:18:41,838 --> 00:18:42,378
[SPEAKER_01]: It's unpleasant.

267
00:18:42,518 --> 00:18:42,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

268
00:18:43,338 --> 00:18:43,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

269
00:18:44,359 --> 00:18:51,301
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you take that feeling for granted, you will take your attitude towards that feeling for granted.

270
00:18:51,341 --> 00:18:53,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling of this pleasure is taken for granted.

271
00:18:53,522 --> 00:18:55,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Me not wanting this pleasure is taken for granted.

272
00:18:56,323 --> 00:18:57,043
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, the

273
00:18:57,770 --> 00:19:07,900
[SPEAKER_01]: The output of that attitude is going to be envy, ill-will, sensuality, for that matter, any of it.

274
00:19:08,501 --> 00:19:20,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Evil wishes, deceitfulness, everything unwholesome will come simply because you are unaware and muddled in regard to the nature of the present enduring feeling in your own experience.

275
00:19:22,286 --> 00:19:24,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Not yesterday, not tomorrow, not before or after.

276
00:19:24,867 --> 00:19:29,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Right here, right now, while you are being engaging or restraining from engaging in all those acts.

277
00:19:30,950 --> 00:19:43,176
[SPEAKER_01]: So you... if you uproot the aptitude in regard to the presently arisen feeling in your experience, you uproot the whole kind of laid out, paved out ways of the proliferation of unwholesome.

278
00:19:44,256 --> 00:19:50,179
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you go around trying to chase every individual unwholesome and trim it back, yeah, good luck with that.

279
00:19:50,880 --> 00:19:51,640
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the Buddha said,

280
00:19:52,738 --> 00:19:55,700
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a palm stump killed at its root.

281
00:19:57,521 --> 00:20:00,883
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't go around trimming the branches that have been proliferated this way or that way.

282
00:20:01,583 --> 00:20:04,585
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize, well, that's a waste of effort and certainly not going to solve the problem.

283
00:20:05,785 --> 00:20:10,128
[SPEAKER_01]: You kill its roots and then whatever's been proliferated, you don't care about it.

284
00:20:10,748 --> 00:20:17,451
[SPEAKER_01]: No amount of pathways of ill-will will make a difference when there is no ill-will to go along those pathways.

285
00:20:17,512 --> 00:20:20,133
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's just like a palm stump dried up, cut up,

286
00:20:20,913 --> 00:20:22,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Destroyed at its root.

287
00:20:22,654 --> 00:20:23,675
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't grow again.

288
00:20:27,698 --> 00:20:28,158
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

289
00:20:28,238 --> 00:20:38,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, there was a passage that probably many, many people in the past and in the future would just read over and, of course, you abandon unwholesome, you get joy and stuff.

290
00:20:39,586 --> 00:20:40,626
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole teaching is in it.

291
00:20:41,787 --> 00:20:44,469
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you don't know what unwholesome is, you won't know what wholesome is.

292
00:20:44,909 --> 00:20:47,191
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you then do that question that the Buddha talks about?

293
00:20:48,585 --> 00:20:51,246
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'll tell you how people might think.

294
00:20:51,326 --> 00:20:51,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I don't care.

295
00:20:52,707 --> 00:20:56,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, for example, it's in this, it's in this that...

296
00:20:56,029 --> 00:20:57,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, well, if the sūtra says it, then it's fine.

297
00:20:58,550 --> 00:20:58,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, this...

298
00:20:59,751 --> 00:21:03,233
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what I've just read there, the abandoning of unwholesome.

299
00:21:03,453 --> 00:21:03,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

300
00:21:04,613 --> 00:21:05,094
[SPEAKER_00]: He does this.

301
00:21:06,034 --> 00:21:08,355
[SPEAKER_00]: He abandons wrong view.

302
00:21:09,336 --> 00:21:09,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

303
00:21:10,256 --> 00:21:14,699
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he gets right view and he's happy because he's purified of evil and wholesome states.

304
00:21:15,763 --> 00:21:17,144
[SPEAKER_00]: And, his mind becomes composed.

305
00:21:17,764 --> 00:21:18,664
[SPEAKER_00]: So, samādhi is there.

306
00:21:21,525 --> 00:21:33,890
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as a result of that composure and that abandonment of evil and wholesome states, it goes into hereby it's pervading one quarter with the mind imbued with kindness.

307
00:21:35,991 --> 00:21:38,672
[SPEAKER_00]: With a mind, eh...

308
00:21:39,392 --> 00:21:39,972
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll say what it is.

309
00:21:40,773 --> 00:21:42,433
[SPEAKER_00]: Without hostility and without ill will.

310
00:21:43,754 --> 00:21:43,994
[SPEAKER_00]: But,

311
00:21:45,227 --> 00:21:49,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Somehow, this whole sutta has been turned upside down in a way.

312
00:21:49,648 --> 00:21:54,549
[SPEAKER_00]: First, one assumes, I will abide pervading one quarter with the mind with loving-kindness.

313
00:21:54,569 --> 00:21:55,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure, sure.

314
00:21:55,169 --> 00:21:58,710
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, I will eventually abandon ill-will.

315
00:21:59,290 --> 00:22:00,771
[SPEAKER_00]: I will eventually gain samādhi.

316
00:22:00,791 --> 00:22:03,791
[SPEAKER_00]: I will eventually be purified of all evil and wholesome states.

317
00:22:03,991 --> 00:22:04,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

318
00:22:05,052 --> 00:22:06,652
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you read the sutta,

319
00:22:07,897 --> 00:22:12,279
[SPEAKER_01]: You already need to understand first, fully, what wholesome and unwholesome is.

320
00:22:12,659 --> 00:22:14,620
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to abandon the wrong view fully.

321
00:22:15,101 --> 00:22:23,685
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to become sotāpanna, then be glad, because you're free from unwholesome, and then you pervade your experience with that gladness, which is brahma-vihāras.

322
00:22:23,785 --> 00:22:25,306
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all to make you just recognize.

323
00:22:25,466 --> 00:22:25,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

324
00:22:26,627 --> 00:22:28,287
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a result of right understanding.

325
00:22:28,348 --> 00:22:32,970
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a method or some visualization or whatever, wishful thinking.

326
00:22:33,637 --> 00:22:37,959
[SPEAKER_01]: They will then miraculously cure you from your ill-will and everything else.

327
00:22:37,979 --> 00:22:41,181
[SPEAKER_00]: It's very easy to assume that, okay, now, if I just... Sure.

328
00:22:41,521 --> 00:22:44,683
[SPEAKER_00]: The opposite of ill-will is... No, because people...

329
00:22:44,763 --> 00:22:46,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, because people are used to doing things.

330
00:22:47,625 --> 00:22:48,725
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the ignorant mind does.

331
00:22:49,746 --> 00:22:52,567
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, I'm doing this, I'm doing that, I'm choosing this, I'm choosing that.

332
00:22:53,128 --> 00:22:58,150
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you start telling them, okay, but now you need to understand the nature of your action.

333
00:22:59,291 --> 00:23:00,832
[SPEAKER_00]: And they would say, okay, so what do I do?

334
00:23:01,592 --> 00:23:03,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that is the threshold.

335
00:23:06,818 --> 00:23:09,040
[SPEAKER_01]: What can be done?

336
00:23:09,481 --> 00:23:10,322
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I must do something.

337
00:23:10,342 --> 00:23:11,263
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they might see the point.

338
00:23:11,283 --> 00:23:16,288
[SPEAKER_01]: They might get the hints and see the signs of, ah, understanding the nature of action frees you from action.

339
00:23:16,328 --> 00:23:17,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's insightful.

340
00:23:18,063 --> 00:23:24,565
[SPEAKER_01]: But they're still gonna have trouble finding a direction to go, to see that, without doing it.

341
00:23:25,565 --> 00:23:26,485
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do I do?

342
00:23:26,965 --> 00:23:29,726
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when you come in clear instruction, what do you do?

343
00:23:30,046 --> 00:23:33,647
[SPEAKER_01]: You understand unwholesome, you understand wholesome, you abandon the wrong view.

344
00:23:34,147 --> 00:23:34,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Do that.

345
00:23:35,448 --> 00:23:37,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, but what do I do in order to do that?

346
00:23:38,408 --> 00:23:40,229
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, tell me how to move.

347
00:23:41,749 --> 00:23:44,950
[SPEAKER_01]: And you could see, it's only natural then, to pervert the order and assume.

348
00:23:45,533 --> 00:24:01,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so I will sit in my room, and I will send positive thoughts to my enemies, and I will think well about everyone, and I will be kind, and I will do this, and I will do this, and I will do this, and through that magical doing, I'm gonna have a magical result of being free from action.

349
00:24:02,005 --> 00:24:05,047
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through doing action, I'm gonna free myself from doing action.

350
00:24:06,108 --> 00:24:09,270
[SPEAKER_01]: And they remain oblivious to that blatant contradiction.

351
00:24:11,572 --> 00:24:14,494
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not about what you do, it's whether you understand.

352
00:24:15,627 --> 00:24:16,888
[SPEAKER_01]: The phenomenon of doing.

353
00:24:17,028 --> 00:24:18,850
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in this case, ill-will, ill-will.

354
00:24:19,230 --> 00:24:21,291
[SPEAKER_01]: You know what you should not do.

355
00:24:21,312 --> 00:24:26,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't engage with ill-will at its face value, as in be restrained and develop virtue beforehand.

356
00:24:26,816 --> 00:24:28,877
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can do that type of negative action.

357
00:24:29,878 --> 00:24:32,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Abstain from unwholesome action.

358
00:24:32,560 --> 00:24:33,201
[SPEAKER_01]: You do that.

359
00:24:34,202 --> 00:24:34,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

360
00:24:34,442 --> 00:24:35,683
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do I do next then?

361
00:24:35,983 --> 00:24:39,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, next, ask yourself the questions whether you even understand

362
00:24:40,565 --> 00:24:45,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Things in your experience, presently there, because when you say, oh, my ill-will is sort of...

363
00:24:46,051 --> 00:24:48,954
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm currently restraining my acts of ill-will.

364
00:24:49,735 --> 00:24:54,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Say somebody says that, and if you were to ask them, okay, so what is that ill-will that you designate to it?

365
00:24:56,502 --> 00:25:01,628
[SPEAKER_01]: If they give any answer except the answer that pertains to the present situation, it's going to be the wrong answer.

366
00:25:02,854 --> 00:25:06,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that ill-will is there when they made that designation.

367
00:25:06,657 --> 00:25:10,200
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if they have to resort to theory or explanation, it means they don't see it.

368
00:25:10,640 --> 00:25:14,283
[SPEAKER_01]: They just serve another view that stands in place of it.

369
00:25:14,604 --> 00:25:16,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Another assumption of what ill-will is.

370
00:25:17,526 --> 00:25:22,170
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is, as long as you serve assumptions, you serve upādāna, basically.

371
00:25:22,190 --> 00:25:24,952
[SPEAKER_01]: You serve your own holding through taking things at face value.

372
00:25:27,846 --> 00:25:34,549
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody says, oh, currently I'm experiencing my mind is in a state of ill-will on account of such and such reasons.

373
00:25:35,229 --> 00:25:42,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Put reasons aside, allow the presently arisen ill-will to endure, and then investigate that.

374
00:25:42,913 --> 00:25:50,136
[SPEAKER_01]: Not through theory, not through third-person view, not through psychologizing, but what are the characteristics of this experience as a whole affected by ill-will?

375
00:25:51,816 --> 00:25:53,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Just what we've done ten minutes ago.

376
00:25:54,077 --> 00:25:55,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the first and obvious

377
00:25:56,627 --> 00:25:57,227
[SPEAKER_01]: It's unpleasant.

378
00:25:57,528 --> 00:25:59,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so the characteristic of ill-will is unpleasant.

379
00:26:00,249 --> 00:26:02,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Could I regard that to be a universal characteristic of ill-will?

380
00:26:02,831 --> 00:26:07,333
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, is there sometimes ill-will that I don't feel pressured or sort of affected by?

381
00:26:08,134 --> 00:26:08,694
[SPEAKER_01]: No, there isn't.

382
00:26:09,434 --> 00:26:13,977
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could safely say, you're not ultimately sure, but you don't need to be.

383
00:26:14,519 --> 00:26:17,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Because at least you're thinking on the basic of presently enduring things.

384
00:26:18,701 --> 00:26:23,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Not on the basis of theory, scholastic investigation, mystical, whatever, psychologizing.

385
00:26:23,163 --> 00:26:23,323
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

386
00:26:23,824 --> 00:26:26,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Presently, what you're experiencing, that's what you're trying to clarify to yourself.

387
00:26:28,526 --> 00:26:29,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Just through what it's there.

388
00:26:30,427 --> 00:26:32,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Looking at the signs and tokens of those things.

389
00:26:34,529 --> 00:26:35,029
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you're...

390
00:26:35,049 --> 00:26:35,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so, ill will...

391
00:26:37,235 --> 00:26:38,156
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you...

392
00:26:38,196 --> 00:26:43,099
[SPEAKER_01]: So, my acts on account of ill-will, things I used to do, things that I'm restraining now, and things that I hope I won't do in the future,

393
00:26:59,750 --> 00:27:04,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, an obvious thing that very few people would actually see on the basis of presently enduring things.

394
00:27:04,111 --> 00:27:05,751
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, many people keep the precepts perfectly.

395
00:27:29,760 --> 00:27:32,662
[SPEAKER_01]: And still remain putuginous, completely no understanding.

396
00:27:33,883 --> 00:27:38,947
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, because they haven't understood it, any of it, on the presently enduring basis.

397
00:27:40,208 --> 00:27:46,192
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you start dealing with it, you realize, within two sentences, that's why you must... it's not negotiable.

398
00:27:46,812 --> 00:27:54,198
[SPEAKER_01]: You must restrain first, because it doesn't come from the arisen experience of ill-will, or centrality for that matter.

399
00:27:54,838 --> 00:27:57,020
[SPEAKER_01]: It's my doing on account of it.

400
00:27:57,986 --> 00:28:01,568
[SPEAKER_01]: And if I keep doing that at its face value, how can I free myself?

401
00:28:02,128 --> 00:28:08,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, basically, if I pressure you and you cannot help by responding, I will always know my pressure works.

402
00:28:10,231 --> 00:28:16,014
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to free yourself from my pressure over you, yet you can't help responding.

403
00:28:16,034 --> 00:28:17,235
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a contradiction in terms.

404
00:28:17,855 --> 00:28:22,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you say, well, I want to be free, the fact that you keep responding to my pressure, I know you're not free.

405
00:28:23,977 --> 00:28:28,198
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first, you have to stop responding to the pressure of your will or the pressure of sensuality.

406
00:28:28,238 --> 00:28:30,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, virtue is non-negotiable.

407
00:28:30,458 --> 00:28:31,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Virtue comes first.

408
00:28:33,019 --> 00:28:35,719
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you... then there's already a basis of clarity there.

409
00:28:35,759 --> 00:28:35,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

410
00:28:36,319 --> 00:28:47,941
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why I must... and when there's clarity in regard to restraint, means there is understanding in regard to the nature of restraint, which means you're doing it less blindly now and you're actually seeing how it pertains to the practice.

411
00:28:48,661 --> 00:28:48,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

412
00:28:48,901 --> 00:28:51,642
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not direct and you also understand that.

413
00:28:52,931 --> 00:28:56,294
[SPEAKER_01]: My restraint does not miraculously result in freedom from ill-will.

414
00:28:57,495 --> 00:29:00,237
[SPEAKER_01]: But now you know exactly why is it necessary.

415
00:29:00,838 --> 00:29:04,321
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, so I have to stop giving in to the pressure in order to free myself from the pressure.

416
00:29:04,761 --> 00:29:09,085
[SPEAKER_01]: And you, again, you don't see it as a theory, you see it on a presently enduring basis of that ill-will.

417
00:29:11,046 --> 00:29:12,568
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you can carry on with that questioning.

418
00:29:13,048 --> 00:29:21,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so now that I am restrained, that I have clarified that with relative certainty that universal characteristic of ill-will, it's unpleasant in pressuring me,

419
00:29:24,244 --> 00:29:29,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Go a step further and then, as we've done, so what is it then that's unpleasant in it?

420
00:29:31,668 --> 00:29:37,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there a particular content or just... Do you have a say over which aspect is going to bother you, of that ill-will?

421
00:29:38,212 --> 00:29:41,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you ever say which part will be unpleasant?

422
00:29:41,354 --> 00:29:42,055
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you don't.

423
00:29:42,115 --> 00:29:44,876
[SPEAKER_01]: It just has a reason, has a package, so to speak.

424
00:29:44,976 --> 00:29:46,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Ill-will, unpleasant pressure.

425
00:29:46,717 --> 00:29:48,198
[SPEAKER_01]: On account of this or this, or this or that.

426
00:29:48,278 --> 00:29:52,501
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is, ill-will in its nature pressures you, so it's being felt unpleasantly.

427
00:29:54,049 --> 00:29:58,151
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the experience of ill-will means experience of feeling displeasure.

428
00:30:00,072 --> 00:30:02,553
[SPEAKER_01]: So, really now, you're not concerned with ill-will anymore.

429
00:30:03,034 --> 00:30:09,597
[SPEAKER_01]: You're concerned with the gratuitous attitude of craving for the absence of the disagreeable feeling.

430
00:30:11,433 --> 00:30:14,495
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you carry on the same questioning, as we've done in other talks.

431
00:30:15,095 --> 00:30:16,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so, feeling, feeling, then.

432
00:30:16,696 --> 00:30:18,037
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the feeling when I refer to it?

433
00:30:18,317 --> 00:30:21,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, not before, after, yesterday, tomorrow, a new theory, new suit.

434
00:30:22,300 --> 00:30:26,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Right here, right now, on the basis of ill will, that I've been self-investigating.

435
00:30:26,802 --> 00:30:32,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me ask myself a further question now, that I've discerned there is a feeling behind it, that's where the problem is rooted.

436
00:30:33,026 --> 00:30:34,767
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the presently enduring

437
00:30:35,146 --> 00:30:39,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Disagreeable feeling of a particularly manifested ill will.

438
00:30:39,827 --> 00:30:43,228
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that unpleasant feeling, what is its nature?

439
00:30:45,309 --> 00:30:47,389
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you describe it in most general terms?

440
00:30:48,969 --> 00:30:52,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, the Buddha... No, it doesn't matter what the Buddha said or who said what.

441
00:30:52,630 --> 00:31:01,193
[SPEAKER_01]: For you, right now, that feeling of disagreeable that keeps enduring for while we're talking all of this, how would you describe it in your own terms?

442
00:31:01,393 --> 00:31:02,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't worry about being right or wrong.

443
00:31:06,078 --> 00:31:11,100
[SPEAKER_01]: And as pretty much everybody I would ever ask this question, said, oh, well, it's just there.

444
00:31:11,900 --> 00:31:12,940
[SPEAKER_01]: That would be the common thread.

445
00:31:13,380 --> 00:31:15,521
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, it's just there.

446
00:31:15,561 --> 00:31:20,863
[SPEAKER_01]: If something is just there, you have no idea why or how for whether it's just there.

447
00:31:21,523 --> 00:31:24,104
[SPEAKER_01]: You are subjected to it.

448
00:31:24,124 --> 00:31:34,747
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if something that's just there, that you're just subjected to it, that you can only sort of act secondarily, so to speak, in response to, but you can't prevent from arising,

449
00:31:35,620 --> 00:31:36,960
[SPEAKER_00]: Why are you regarding that thing as yours?

450
00:31:38,781 --> 00:31:44,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are you taking your attitude of response at face value?

451
00:31:45,202 --> 00:31:51,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Because your attitude of response implies that you can do something about that thing just being there oppressing you.

452
00:31:51,804 --> 00:32:02,407
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you look at it closely, you realize, I have even no idea how this thing came to be, how long it will be, let alone how to, like, make it go away when I want to.

453
00:32:03,126 --> 00:32:09,749
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha would say, oh, well, if the pleasantness feeling is yours, how about that unpleasant feeling or neutral feeling?

454
00:32:10,409 --> 00:32:11,350
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, feeling is mine.

455
00:32:11,390 --> 00:32:12,370
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, well, which one?

456
00:32:14,011 --> 00:32:17,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if feeling were truly yours, you would have only one feeling that you want.

457
00:32:18,533 --> 00:32:20,774
[SPEAKER_01]: You would never experience feelings that you don't want.

458
00:32:21,414 --> 00:32:29,298
[SPEAKER_01]: But because more often than not, you experience feelings that you don't want, you realize that when you want feelings, you actually realize, oh, actually, even that is not in my control.

459
00:32:30,978 --> 00:32:32,439
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the nature of sensuality.

460
00:32:34,922 --> 00:32:40,445
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all rooted in the same response towards a reasoned feeling, craving, response of craving.

461
00:32:40,945 --> 00:32:51,971
[SPEAKER_01]: And response, where I use the word response, it's not a process, it's not a response in time, it's a simultaneously present attitude to the simultaneously present phenomenon of that particular feeling.

462
00:32:54,512 --> 00:32:58,454
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, it shouldn't be there, but for Putujana, it is there.

463
00:32:59,054 --> 00:33:00,235
[SPEAKER_01]: That is his starting point.

464
00:33:01,013 --> 00:33:03,954
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether it's ill-will, whether it's any envy, what is that?

465
00:33:04,195 --> 00:33:08,397
[SPEAKER_01]: You bring it down and it's going to come down to how it's being felt, that experience as a whole.

466
00:33:08,937 --> 00:33:14,500
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's going to come down to whether you're trying to indulge the unwholesome, get rid of it, or distract yourself from it.

467
00:33:15,400 --> 00:33:17,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Greed, lust, aversion, delusion.

468
00:33:19,002 --> 00:33:24,365
[SPEAKER_01]: If you remove lust, aversion, delusion, you've removed the entire universe of unwholesome.

469
00:33:24,445 --> 00:33:27,286
[SPEAKER_01]: Possibilities of unwholesome cannot manifest then.

470
00:33:29,883 --> 00:33:36,793
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so, one sees it, oh, I can't, I can't see any craving, yeah, in regard to fear.

471
00:33:36,813 --> 00:33:38,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because you don't see the feeling.

472
00:33:38,355 --> 00:33:42,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, so, if you say, I can't see any craving, the answer, basically, can be only two answers.

473
00:33:42,801 --> 00:33:43,963
[SPEAKER_01]: You're either an arahant,

474
00:33:44,935 --> 00:33:46,056
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no third option.

475
00:33:46,096 --> 00:33:51,059
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is in common to resisting the feeling, welcoming the feeling, covering up the feeling?

476
00:33:51,139 --> 00:33:53,180
[SPEAKER_00]: What's in common to these three things?

477
00:33:54,081 --> 00:33:54,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Except feeling.

478
00:34:13,683 --> 00:34:14,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

479
00:34:14,363 --> 00:34:14,964
[SPEAKER_01]: So, craving.

480
00:34:14,984 --> 00:34:15,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Fine.

481
00:34:15,964 --> 00:34:20,326
[SPEAKER_01]: But, craving, for many people, they think they understood craving, they understood the word craving.

482
00:34:20,686 --> 00:34:26,268
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how would you then describe that craving that's in common to these three attitudes towards the feeling?

483
00:34:26,509 --> 00:34:30,410
[SPEAKER_01]: What would be the characteristics of the craving?

484
00:34:31,991 --> 00:34:38,594
[SPEAKER_00]: An attitude of... an attitude of pressure, some kind.

485
00:34:46,428 --> 00:34:47,709
[SPEAKER_01]: See, like, that's normal.

486
00:34:48,129 --> 00:34:50,030
[SPEAKER_01]: And people will stop and think that.

487
00:34:50,711 --> 00:34:52,972
[SPEAKER_01]: Go back to the present enduring feeling.

488
00:34:52,992 --> 00:34:53,953
[SPEAKER_01]: How am I feeling right now?

489
00:34:54,373 --> 00:34:55,173
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm feeling neutral.

490
00:34:55,514 --> 00:35:03,639
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the Buddha said, in regard to neutral feeling, there is a tendency towards ignorance, towards distraction, towards oblivion.

491
00:35:04,899 --> 00:35:08,962
[SPEAKER_01]: And the tendency is where the craving is.

492
00:35:09,002 --> 00:35:11,604
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what would be then, in your own words,

493
00:35:12,416 --> 00:35:16,878
[SPEAKER_01]: The characteristic of that tendency of the kind of present enduring feeling.

494
00:35:16,958 --> 00:35:24,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're not necessarily doing the distraction, you can recognize the tendency to do so, in regard to neither unpleasant or pleasant feeling.

495
00:35:27,542 --> 00:35:29,402
[SPEAKER_00]: I feel wanting, just wanting to...

496
00:35:29,522 --> 00:35:31,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Wanting, but wanting is already the result of it.

497
00:35:32,103 --> 00:35:32,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

498
00:35:32,664 --> 00:35:41,767
[SPEAKER_01]: But, so, when you want something different from what it is now, enduring, how would you describe that?

499
00:35:42,552 --> 00:35:43,272
[SPEAKER_00]: We're resisting.

500
00:35:43,953 --> 00:35:44,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Who resists?

501
00:35:44,553 --> 00:35:44,753
[SPEAKER_01]: What?

502
00:35:46,374 --> 00:35:47,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Me, resisting.

503
00:35:48,275 --> 00:35:50,296
[SPEAKER_01]: You're resisting the reason-feeling.

504
00:35:52,997 --> 00:35:54,978
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, what do you do?

505
00:35:54,998 --> 00:35:55,758
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you want to do?

506
00:35:59,020 --> 00:35:59,900
[SPEAKER_00]: I want it to change.

507
00:36:00,360 --> 00:36:01,241
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to replace it.

508
00:36:01,301 --> 00:36:02,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly.

509
00:36:03,122 --> 00:36:10,105
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you go back to see what you're resisting, as we've just done here, you realize it has a reason on its own, enduring.

510
00:36:11,049 --> 00:36:12,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, you want to override it.

511
00:36:14,811 --> 00:36:16,613
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize how futile that is.

512
00:36:18,114 --> 00:36:23,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the only way you can override something is if you have a say.

513
00:36:23,957 --> 00:36:32,083
[SPEAKER_01]: But if something is just there on its own, oppressing you, or isn't on its own, enduring on its own, you are subjected to it, it's not subjected to you.

514
00:36:33,964 --> 00:36:40,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are you entertaining notions of overriding it, when you can't even conceive where or how that would be possible?

515
00:36:41,901 --> 00:36:48,124
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you realize you can entertain notions of overriding it by not understanding what it is that you resist and try to override.

516
00:36:48,465 --> 00:36:52,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, ignorance results in that craving, or rather, ignorance fuels the craving.

517
00:36:56,329 --> 00:37:03,713
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you stop ignoring the nature of that which is there that you're trying to resist, can you then still engage in the acts of craving?

518
00:37:05,188 --> 00:37:06,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, and it's not...

519
00:37:06,569 --> 00:37:13,695
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't think there would be a... you wouldn't have to try to un-engage.

520
00:37:13,895 --> 00:37:14,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

521
00:37:14,676 --> 00:37:19,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you don't ignore it, there is no room for craving or non-craving.

522
00:37:19,359 --> 00:37:21,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, well, it's absence of craving, basically.

523
00:37:21,901 --> 00:37:25,024
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no room for wanting or not wanting, in that sense.

524
00:37:25,845 --> 00:37:27,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Because both can be a craving.

525
00:37:27,586 --> 00:37:28,847
[SPEAKER_01]: I want this, I don't want this.

526
00:37:29,007 --> 00:37:29,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you crave.

527
00:37:31,024 --> 00:37:46,380
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as we just clarified, again, on the basis of the present enduring feeling, if you stop ignoring the obvious universal nature of that enduring phenomenon that you're feeling,

528
00:37:47,706 --> 00:37:50,027
[SPEAKER_01]: You will then stop entertaining the notions of craving.

529
00:37:50,148 --> 00:37:51,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, no matter what you feel.

530
00:37:51,648 --> 00:37:52,929
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter what you feel.

531
00:37:52,949 --> 00:37:55,391
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you've understood the nature of feeling in general.

532
00:37:55,531 --> 00:37:58,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you stopped ignoring the right nature of feeling.

533
00:37:58,152 --> 00:38:02,115
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's when people are mindfulness and then like touching, moving, moving, looking, looking.

534
00:38:02,435 --> 00:38:05,297
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not mindfulness of everything and nothing at the same time.

535
00:38:06,217 --> 00:38:08,999
[SPEAKER_01]: It's mindfulness of feelings, thoughts or intentions.

536
00:38:09,019 --> 00:38:12,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, they're going to boil down to feeling and craving towards feelings.

537
00:38:13,518 --> 00:38:17,259
[SPEAKER_01]: So, mindfulness of the present enduring feeling in the background of your experience.

538
00:38:17,319 --> 00:38:18,220
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the present mood?

539
00:38:18,240 --> 00:38:19,260
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the basis of it?

540
00:38:19,720 --> 00:38:21,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it pleasant, unpleasant, neutral?

541
00:38:21,521 --> 00:38:22,441
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your starting point.

542
00:38:22,601 --> 00:38:24,502
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you don't lose the sight of.

543
00:38:25,062 --> 00:38:28,464
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't lose the sight of it, you cannot ignore its nature.

544
00:38:28,524 --> 00:38:35,686
[SPEAKER_01]: And the nature of these things that have a reason for you, the Buddha said himself.

545
00:38:35,807 --> 00:38:36,987
[SPEAKER_01]: It's anicca and dukkha.

546
00:38:39,473 --> 00:38:42,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, then, your experience will be anatta, if you see that nature.

547
00:38:42,495 --> 00:38:43,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, if you see the nature...

548
00:38:44,797 --> 00:38:52,202
[SPEAKER_01]: But, basically, like, all of these attitudes of resisting, craving, they all come from the view of, I am in charge, I am in control.

549
00:38:53,844 --> 00:38:55,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that, I will replace this.

550
00:38:55,785 --> 00:38:57,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that, I will make this stay longer.

551
00:38:58,607 --> 00:39:02,010
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you could see, like, even that I am in control and I will do all of this,

552
00:39:03,053 --> 00:39:06,815
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because of your ignorance of what you're trying to replace.

553
00:39:07,015 --> 00:39:08,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance of the nature of it.

554
00:39:09,316 --> 00:39:15,159
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, if you press yourself far enough in the questioning, the answer is going to be, oh, well, it's just there.

555
00:39:15,799 --> 00:39:23,843
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know how, why, how long, for what reason, the feeling is just there and it's felt like this.

556
00:39:25,164 --> 00:39:26,945
[SPEAKER_01]: It oppresses me, I don't oppress it.

557
00:39:28,892 --> 00:39:29,933
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm subjected to it.

558
00:39:30,573 --> 00:39:44,024
[SPEAKER_01]: And you cannot even conceive, even people who are full-on, out of other, believe in their self, self-centered, whatever, megalomaniac, even they could not ignore that.

559
00:39:44,705 --> 00:39:51,810
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that all of your sense of self and your responses are secondary to the fact the thing has arisen first on its own.

560
00:39:52,351 --> 00:39:53,432
[SPEAKER_00]: And you are subjected to it.

561
00:39:53,512 --> 00:39:54,232
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what Nietzsche

562
00:39:56,240 --> 00:39:57,521
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly.

563
00:39:57,561 --> 00:40:01,523
[SPEAKER_01]: So, recognizing the characteristics of these things that determine you.

564
00:40:02,403 --> 00:40:10,328
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said, um, sabe saṅkāra anicca, sabe saṅkāra dukkha, sabe dhamma anatta.

565
00:40:11,428 --> 00:40:14,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the self-view, sense of self, is on the level of dhamma.

566
00:40:15,731 --> 00:40:18,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Not on the level... well, it's related to the level of saṅkāra, but it's not there.

567
00:40:18,912 --> 00:40:23,395
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like everything is impermanent, everything is suffering, everything is not self.

568
00:40:24,097 --> 00:40:26,058
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's a third-person view about everything.

569
00:40:27,159 --> 00:40:36,125
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you want to see that presently arisen things for me, that determine me, are arisen on its own, and they're oppressing me.

570
00:40:36,666 --> 00:40:40,208
[SPEAKER_01]: And those things I've been talking about, that's exactly what anicca and dukkha is.

571
00:40:41,008 --> 00:40:44,031
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, like, pleasure is oppressing you.

572
00:40:44,851 --> 00:40:47,473
[SPEAKER_01]: Dukkha is not like pain and pain everywhere, just pain.

573
00:40:49,134 --> 00:40:50,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Dukkha is being oppressed.

574
00:40:51,435 --> 00:40:55,198
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the questioning we've been doing, again, the entire dhamma is contained in it.

575
00:40:55,338 --> 00:40:56,299
[SPEAKER_01]: All of it.

576
00:40:56,319 --> 00:40:57,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Anicca, dukkha, whatever.

577
00:40:57,340 --> 00:40:58,921
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you could see, if you discern

578
00:41:15,880 --> 00:41:23,865
[SPEAKER_01]: The peripheral feeling, the mood that you are in, whatever you're doing particularly, this or that, we're not talking, the background of your mood, what is... what is the fundamental?

579
00:41:23,905 --> 00:41:24,645
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you label it?

580
00:41:25,126 --> 00:41:26,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Positive, negative, or maybe neutral?

581
00:41:27,507 --> 00:41:28,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's a bit positive.

582
00:41:28,828 --> 00:41:29,388
[SPEAKER_01]: I feel okay.

583
00:41:29,468 --> 00:41:32,850
[SPEAKER_01]: I feel light and easy, whatever else, means it's pleasant.

584
00:41:34,111 --> 00:41:35,592
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that is universal nature.

585
00:41:36,372 --> 00:41:40,715
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that pleasure and use can endure for days sometimes, even in Putujana's mind.

586
00:41:41,664 --> 00:41:42,624
[SPEAKER_01]: Or something bothersome.

587
00:41:42,684 --> 00:41:45,365
[SPEAKER_01]: This disagreeable feeling can fundamentally endure for days.

588
00:41:46,025 --> 00:41:55,708
[SPEAKER_01]: On a particular level, you can have this pleasant feeling and this pleasant, but they're particular when you're looking at the most general nature of what's overall being felt.

589
00:41:58,409 --> 00:41:59,649
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where the craving is.

590
00:42:00,090 --> 00:42:08,032
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you stop ignoring what's overall being felt, and in order to stop ignoring that, you have to stop giving in to the particular pursuits

591
00:42:09,821 --> 00:42:20,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you will be aware of your mood.

592
00:42:21,529 --> 00:42:25,811
[SPEAKER_01]: You will be aware of the enduring feeling on that fundamental basis.

593
00:42:26,572 --> 00:42:36,318
[SPEAKER_01]: The more you are aware of the right... the more you're aware of the feeling on that right level, the less you will be able to entertain things that directly

594
00:42:38,535 --> 00:42:45,761
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basic composure of mindfulness.

595
00:42:46,162 --> 00:42:52,267
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to do a proper vipassana, then, on the basis of that composure, you ask these questions we've just done.

596
00:42:53,388 --> 00:42:55,409
[SPEAKER_01]: What would be the universal characteristic?

597
00:42:55,730 --> 00:42:57,892
[SPEAKER_01]: How would I describe this enduring feeling?

598
00:42:58,532 --> 00:43:00,694
[SPEAKER_01]: It clearly now endures for two or three days.

599
00:43:00,754 --> 00:43:01,595
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not saying it.

600
00:43:02,095 --> 00:43:04,597
[SPEAKER_01]: It just happens to be pleasant, so I don't feel threatened by it.

601
00:43:04,778 --> 00:43:04,998
[SPEAKER_01]: But,

602
00:43:06,249 --> 00:43:09,733
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't feel threatened by the particular pleasure of that feeling.

603
00:43:10,774 --> 00:43:11,775
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to look beyond.

604
00:43:12,135 --> 00:43:12,536
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

605
00:43:12,576 --> 00:43:13,416
[SPEAKER_01]: What's universal?

606
00:43:13,517 --> 00:43:18,662
[SPEAKER_01]: What is... You can ask yourself, what is in common to this fundamentally pleasant mood that I have?

607
00:43:19,202 --> 00:43:24,908
[SPEAKER_01]: What's in common to this and to unpleasant mood that I had or will have in the future?

608
00:43:25,449 --> 00:43:26,470
[SPEAKER_01]: Or the neutral mood?

609
00:43:27,120 --> 00:43:29,681
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you stop being concerned with pleasant, unpleasant, neutral.

610
00:43:30,041 --> 00:43:33,441
[SPEAKER_01]: You start looking at the nature of that feeling, that is pleasant, unpleasant.

611
00:43:33,881 --> 00:43:36,322
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, again, you don't need to, oh, it's anicca, it's anicca.

612
00:43:36,382 --> 00:43:36,522
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

613
00:43:37,262 --> 00:43:39,723
[SPEAKER_01]: If you say that, then ask yourself, what is anicca for me?

614
00:43:39,883 --> 00:43:41,303
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I designating by anicca?

615
00:43:41,663 --> 00:43:46,584
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to discern the characteristics of that feeling, and those characteristics will be anicca and dukkha.

616
00:43:47,044 --> 00:43:49,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the Buddha himself said, he didn't invent the dhamma.

617
00:43:50,385 --> 00:43:51,965
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't... he created this.

618
00:43:52,146 --> 00:43:53,146
[SPEAKER_01]: He rediscovered it.

619
00:43:53,246 --> 00:43:54,206
[SPEAKER_01]: It was always there.

620
00:43:55,035 --> 00:43:57,636
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why freedom from ignorance is possible.

621
00:43:58,636 --> 00:44:06,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, if you look at these things genuinely, correctly, sufficiently, that is their nature.

622
00:44:07,378 --> 00:44:08,178
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just there.

623
00:44:09,259 --> 00:44:11,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, I'm not its creator.

624
00:44:11,939 --> 00:44:15,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, it's just there now, but when it isn't, it won't be.

625
00:44:15,820 --> 00:44:17,701
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, it's permanent, impermanent.

626
00:44:18,061 --> 00:44:19,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, it cannot be owned.

627
00:44:20,761 --> 00:44:22,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, it's just there.

628
00:44:22,142 --> 00:44:23,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I bring it into me?

629
00:44:23,843 --> 00:44:25,963
[SPEAKER_01]: and assimilate its whole domain, make it mine.

630
00:44:26,024 --> 00:44:26,444
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

631
00:44:27,204 --> 00:44:28,184
[SPEAKER_01]: You can get closer to it.

632
00:44:28,204 --> 00:44:30,965
[SPEAKER_01]: You can have this attitude or that attitude, more attitude, less attitude.

633
00:44:31,825 --> 00:44:39,787
[SPEAKER_01]: For the duration of any of that, that thing will always be there on its own, enduring.

634
00:44:39,807 --> 00:44:45,728
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the... what is the... what is... what is another characteristic of that... what is the universal characteristic of

635
00:44:46,858 --> 00:44:49,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasant feeling, unpleasant feeling, or neutral feeling.

636
00:44:49,639 --> 00:44:54,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in theory, but on the basis of pleasant feeling that you're currently feeling.

637
00:44:54,180 --> 00:44:54,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasant mood.

638
00:44:58,321 --> 00:44:59,342
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just there.

639
00:44:59,502 --> 00:45:00,082
[SPEAKER_01]: We said that.

640
00:45:01,883 --> 00:45:02,843
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no idea.

641
00:45:03,863 --> 00:45:13,666
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't even conceive where it... how the feeling... You can explain using modern science and whatnot, but all of that is secondary to the fact that this thing is just there.

642
00:45:13,986 --> 00:45:16,367
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't go through it and see what's behind the feeling.

643
00:45:17,351 --> 00:45:18,332
[SPEAKER_01]: That is your bottom.

644
00:45:18,652 --> 00:45:20,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I have no choice in it.

645
00:45:20,774 --> 00:45:26,698
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no choice in the matter, which means, who is, who is basically subordinate to who?

646
00:45:28,159 --> 00:45:29,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Are your feelings subordinate to you?

647
00:45:30,761 --> 00:45:36,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Or actually, your own choice and intention, volition is subordinate to the present-realism feeling that's just there.

648
00:45:37,146 --> 00:45:40,188
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it oppresses you, it controls you, and that's unpleasant.

649
00:45:41,142 --> 00:45:49,706
[SPEAKER_01]: So, we already described anicca and dukkha on account of the characteristics that anybody who starts this questioning can start seeing on account of presently enduring feeling.

650
00:45:50,747 --> 00:45:57,710
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, I insist presently enduring feeling must be on the level of the most general background of the mood that you are in.

651
00:45:58,391 --> 00:46:01,912
[SPEAKER_01]: Not on account of, oh, I'm looking this, I'm feeling light in my body and stuff.

652
00:46:02,273 --> 00:46:04,274
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is secondary to a sense of self.

653
00:46:05,494 --> 00:46:06,755
[SPEAKER_01]: But mood never is.

654
00:46:07,720 --> 00:46:18,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can, you can, you can, like, tell yourself you're a master controller of your experience, but moods, fundamentally good, bad or neutral, is something that always comes underneath you.

655
00:46:19,024 --> 00:46:29,187
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, if you go that far to discern the nature of the feeling, or you have to go that far to discern the nature of the feeling, because that's the way that you cannot re-pervert it back, so to speak, to being mine.

656
00:46:29,967 --> 00:46:31,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Any lesser feeling than that,

657
00:46:32,833 --> 00:46:38,454
[SPEAKER_01]: Can be appropriated easily because your sense of self remains kind of impartial to it.

658
00:46:39,974 --> 00:46:41,815
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not really the feeling you're looking for.

659
00:46:45,555 --> 00:46:48,516
[SPEAKER_01]: The fundamentally, is it positive, negative or neutral?

660
00:46:49,176 --> 00:46:50,536
[SPEAKER_00]: That you have no say in.

661
00:46:56,057 --> 00:47:02,178
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will arrive at that through ill-will, questioning the ill-will, questioning anything unwholesome, questioning centrality,

662
00:47:04,170 --> 00:47:04,430
[SPEAKER_01]: All right.

663
00:47:06,210 --> 00:47:09,011
[SPEAKER_01]: We'll arrive at the same point.

664
00:47:09,031 --> 00:47:22,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing saṅkharas as anicca, as oppressing your dukkha, and then, automatically, you will not be able to entertain the notion that you are master controller of your whole experience, when you realize you are the sub... you are the one subjected to it.

665
00:47:24,233 --> 00:47:30,414
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, that experience of I'm the master controller is seen, oh, this is not my... this is not the master and controller.

666
00:47:31,715 --> 00:47:33,735
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that experience becomes the experience of anatta.

667
00:47:35,430 --> 00:47:47,240
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's where anicca is.

668
00:47:47,780 --> 00:47:50,002
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like what your self sees.

669
00:47:50,022 --> 00:47:53,685
[SPEAKER_01]: It's what undermines your sense of self, which is the five aggregates.

670
00:47:54,365 --> 00:48:03,673
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why somebody who has discerned this, as we would say, would just stay mindful of manifestation of the aggregates coming and going, arising and passing, or whatever translation is.

671
00:48:05,458 --> 00:48:07,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what the characteristics are.

672
00:48:08,043 --> 00:48:10,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Through staying mindful of it, you cannot ignore them.

673
00:48:10,729 --> 00:48:13,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Through not ignoring them, you cannot proliferate craving.

674
00:48:13,955 --> 00:48:16,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Through not proliferating craving, you cannot suffer anymore.

675
00:48:20,230 --> 00:48:21,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with, like, intentions.

676
00:48:22,011 --> 00:48:22,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

677
00:48:22,952 --> 00:48:26,054
[SPEAKER_00]: You find there is an intention there, towards...

678
00:48:26,074 --> 00:48:27,375
[SPEAKER_01]: Same with any aggregate, basically.

679
00:48:27,716 --> 00:48:31,458
[SPEAKER_00]: But, obviously, the feeling is where things converge, so to speak.

680
00:48:31,598 --> 00:48:34,200
[SPEAKER_00]: You intend here and there because you feel.

681
00:48:35,381 --> 00:48:41,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if people were not starting with wrong views, then, in theory, they could pick up any aggregate and question it rightly.

682
00:48:42,246 --> 00:48:48,351
[SPEAKER_01]: But everybody starts with wrong views, and the convergence of their wrong views is on craving, and that's where the feeling is.

683
00:48:49,271 --> 00:49:00,394
[SPEAKER_01]: So, unless you start undoing it from that basis, and you, say, start thinking about intentions of the nature of viññāṇa and whatnot, you're not going to be thinking on the present of presently enduring phenomena.

684
00:49:01,754 --> 00:49:03,154
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can, theoretically, sure.

685
00:49:03,274 --> 00:49:05,235
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say consciousness-consciousness.

686
00:49:06,015 --> 00:49:15,497
[SPEAKER_01]: When I say consciousness, in English or in Pali or whatever language, what am I referring to in this experience, right here, right now, while I'm saying that question?

687
00:49:16,878 --> 00:49:17,758
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I referring to?

688
00:49:18,711 --> 00:49:19,211
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what is it?

689
00:49:19,891 --> 00:49:21,232
[SPEAKER_01]: Tell me the characteristics of it.

690
00:49:22,492 --> 00:49:24,653
[SPEAKER_01]: But that would be much harder, much more subtle.

691
00:49:24,693 --> 00:49:28,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind you, consciousness is the most refined of all, as the Buddha said himself.

692
00:49:28,974 --> 00:49:37,457
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if one does that on the basis of the presently enduring mood and feeling, then it becomes actually fully intelligible.

693
00:49:38,777 --> 00:49:39,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you know it.

694
00:49:39,418 --> 00:49:40,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is what needs to be done.

695
00:49:42,219 --> 00:49:46,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Most people, most people's relation with Buddhist practice is magical almost.

696
00:49:47,948 --> 00:50:01,217
[SPEAKER_01]: I believe that through being virtuous, I believe that through being good, I believe that through doing this method and technique, I believe through the reading suttas, I'm going to have the experience of enlightenment.

697
00:50:01,237 --> 00:50:01,678
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like...

698
00:50:01,698 --> 00:50:02,718
[SPEAKER_01]: I believe in rebirth.

699
00:50:03,579 --> 00:50:04,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I believe in everything basically.

700
00:50:04,720 --> 00:50:08,582
[SPEAKER_01]: I believe that me doing this to the left is going to result in these things to the right.

701
00:50:09,203 --> 00:50:14,847
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no connection between the two that I can see, or that I don't have to fabricate for myself.

702
00:50:17,421 --> 00:50:27,230
[SPEAKER_01]: The definition of the one who is free from wrong view is he knows wholesome as wholesome and unwholesome as unwholesome.

703
00:50:28,030 --> 00:50:30,612
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he knows why good is good and bad is bad.

704
00:50:30,913 --> 00:50:32,254
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not just believing in it.

705
00:50:34,336 --> 00:50:38,139
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why he's free from Sīla Bhāgavatam's virtue and duty.

706
00:50:40,341 --> 00:50:45,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's my duty, you know, to sit and watch my nostrils because how else can I become enlightened?

707
00:50:45,550 --> 00:50:46,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but why is that your duty?

708
00:50:47,470 --> 00:50:48,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Ask yourself that.

709
00:50:51,511 --> 00:50:57,312
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like through fabricating answers based on, you know, the commentarial text you read and just providing back the information.

710
00:50:57,392 --> 00:50:57,552
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

711
00:50:58,292 --> 00:51:03,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Presently arisen cause has been presently understood and that's the freedom from suffering.

712
00:51:04,093 --> 00:51:04,933
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha said.

713
00:51:06,734 --> 00:51:12,655
[SPEAKER_01]: So, do you understand presently arisen things and does that understanding presently, simultaneously, free you from them?

714
00:51:18,797 --> 00:51:22,718
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you never start questioning it on that level, how do you expect to find it out?

715
00:51:24,758 --> 00:51:26,279
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Buddha said, deal with the knife.

716
00:51:26,659 --> 00:51:27,359
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, keep digging.

717
00:51:28,419 --> 00:51:30,520
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, keep digging back or in.

718
00:51:31,560 --> 00:51:37,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Undoing the layers of your views and what you think phenomena are and feelings are and intentions are.

719
00:51:38,222 --> 00:51:43,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just, oh, keep fabricating further and you'll arrive at the right goal through your state of putusha.

720
00:51:44,896 --> 00:51:51,822
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way to arrive at the goal is to undo, dismantle, stop feeding, stop providing nutrients for your state of putuji.

721
00:51:53,743 --> 00:51:58,868
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's usually completely the opposite direction.

722
00:51:59,268 --> 00:52:06,434
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, that's always the completely opposite direction from where people usually tend to go in their practice.

723
00:52:16,894 --> 00:52:20,476
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even when somebody approaches the sutta and says, oh, yeah, yeah, this, this, this, this makes sense, makes sense.

724
00:52:21,217 --> 00:52:25,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead of building further, you have to look back now.

725
00:52:25,800 --> 00:52:27,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so I think I understood this sutta.

726
00:52:30,043 --> 00:52:31,584
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I a sotāpanna as a result of it?

727
00:52:31,744 --> 00:52:36,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Because truly understanding a sutta will mean at least the right view.

728
00:52:37,188 --> 00:52:37,388
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

729
00:52:37,728 --> 00:52:38,249
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

730
00:52:38,349 --> 00:52:39,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it the sutta's fault?

731
00:52:40,771 --> 00:52:41,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it translation's fault?

732
00:52:41,932 --> 00:52:43,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, maybe to some extent.

733
00:52:43,893 --> 00:52:45,454
[SPEAKER_01]: The translation's fault, not the sutta's fault.

734
00:52:45,874 --> 00:52:51,657
[SPEAKER_01]: But really, it's your preconceived notions of the terms that you're reading in the sutta.

735
00:52:53,298 --> 00:52:54,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Those notions are wrong.

736
00:52:55,199 --> 00:52:58,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that, your usage of the meanings in the sutta is wrong.

737
00:53:01,663 --> 00:53:06,066
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you start undoing your notions of the wrong terms with the help of the suttas.

738
00:53:07,006 --> 00:53:08,227
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you get to read them rightly.

